@startuml

header "RabbitMQ Middle-Man: Private Group Message (Instance 1 → Instance 2)"
footer "Author: Tuzaku | User2=group 42, User3=group 45 (same instance)"

actor "User1\n(Instance 1,\ngroup 42)" as User1
actor "User2\n(Instance 2,\ngroup 42)" as User2
actor "User3\n(Instance 2,\ngroup 45)" as User3

box "Spring Boot Instance 1" #AliceBlue
  participant "WebSocketController" as Ctrl1
  participant "CustomRabbitMQ\nBrokerHandler" as Handler1
  participant "DynamicRabbitMQ\nListener" as Listener1
  queue "ws.instance-1.inbound" as Q1
end box

box "RabbitMQ Broker" #LightYellow
  participant "Topic Exchange\n**chat.groups**" as Exchange
end box

box "Spring Boot Instance 2" #HoneyDew
  queue "ws.instance-2.inbound" as Q2
  participant "DynamicRabbitMQ\nListener" as Listener2
  participant "CustomRabbitMQ\nBrokerHandler" as Handler2
  participant "SimpleBroker" as SimpleBroker2
end box

note over Handler1, Handler2
  **RabbitMQ mapping (group chat):**
  | STOMP destination   | Exchange      | Routing key |
  | /topic/group.42     | chat.groups   | group.42    |
  | /topic/group.45     | chat.groups   | group.45    |

  **Per-instance queue (fixed, created at startup):**
  - Instance 1: ws.instance-1.inbound
  - Instance 2: ws.instance-2.inbound
  **Bindings are dynamic** — one binding per active STOMP destination on that instance queue.
end note

== A. Topology before any subscriptions ==

note over Exchange, Q2
  At startup (both instances):
  - Exchange **chat.groups** is declared (durable topic exchange)
  - Queue **ws.instance-1.inbound** exists, consumer running
  - Queue **ws.instance-2.inbound** exists, consumer running
  - **No bindings yet** → publish to chat.groups would match nothing
end note

== B. Users on Instance 2 subscribe to their groups ==

User2 -> Handler2: STOMP SUBSCRIBE /topic/group.42
Handler2 -> Exchange: **declareBinding**\nchat.groups ---[**group.42**]-> ws.instance-2.inbound
Handler2 -> SimpleBroker2: register User2 on /topic/group.42

User3 -> Handler2: STOMP SUBSCRIBE /topic/group.45
Handler2 -> Exchange: **declareBinding**\nchat.groups ---[**group.45**]-> ws.instance-2.inbound
Handler2 -> SimpleBroker2: register User3 on /topic/group.45

note over Q2
  Same queue, **two bindings** (different routing keys):
  - chat.groups ---[group.42]-> ws.instance-2.inbound
  - chat.groups ---[group.45]-> ws.instance-2.inbound
end note

note over Q1
  Instance 1 queue has **no bindings** in this example.
end note

== C. User1 on Instance 1 sends a group-42 message (PUBLISH) ==

User1 -> Ctrl1: send message to group 42
Ctrl1 -> Handler1: publishToRabbitMQ("/topic/group.42", payload)
Handler1 -> Handler1: routing key = **group.42**
Handler1 -> Handler1: headers:\nsource-instance-id = instance-1\nstomp-destination = /topic/group.42
Handler1 -> Exchange: **basic.publish**\nexchange = chat.groups\nrouting key = **group.42**

note over Exchange
  Topic exchange matches **group.42** only against bindings.
  - binding **group.42** on ws.instance-2.inbound → **MATCH** ✓
  - binding **group.45** on ws.instance-2.inbound → **no match** ✗
end note

Exchange -> Q2: deliver message\n(matched: group.42 binding only)
note over Q1 #White
  ws.instance-1.inbound: no group.42 binding → **not delivered**
end note

== D. Instance 2 consumes — only group-42 subscribers receive it ==

Q2 -> Listener2: onMessage()
Listener2 -> Listener2: source-instance-id = instance-1\n≠ instance-2 → forward
Listener2 -> Listener2: stomp-destination = /topic/group.42
Listener2 -> SimpleBroker2: convertAndSend("/topic/group.42", payload)

SimpleBroker2 -> User2: MESSAGE ✓\n(subscribed to /topic/group.42)

note over User3 #MistyRose
  User3 does **NOT** receive this message.
  Two independent reasons:
  1. **RabbitMQ:** routing key group.42 did not match the group.45 binding (User3's group).
  2. **SimpleBroker:** even after consume, delivery goes only to /topic/group.42 subscribers — User3 is on /topic/group.45.
end note

User3 -> User3: no message ✗

== E. What if Instance 1 also had a group-42 binding? ==

note over Exchange, Listener1
  If a user on Instance 1 were also subscribed to /topic/group.42,
  RabbitMQ would also deliver a copy to **ws.instance-1.inbound**
  (matched binding: group.42).

  Instance 1 listener checks **source-instance-id**:
  - source = instance-1 → **skip** (local users already got it via SimpleBroker)

  User3 on Instance 2 is still unaffected — group.45 binding never matches a group.42 publish.
end note

@enduml
